+  **Root cause (confirmed):** `fd_dup()` (`src/fd/fd_dup.c`), used for
+  every `[n]>&m` redirection including persistent ("exec") ones, does
+  NOT take an independent copy of the source fd's state — it copies
+  *pointers* into the source struct's own embedded buffer storage
+  (`d->r = dupe->r; d->w = dupe->w; d->dup = dupe;`) and leaves the
+  real kernel dup2() to happen lazily later (`fdtable_dup()`,
+  triggered by `fdtable_exec()`/`fdtable_lazy()`). For `exec >&2
+  2>/dev/null`, by the time that lazy resolution actually runs, the
+  *second* redirection has already run `fd_new(2,...)` ->
+  `fdtable_newfd(2,...)` -> `fd_reinit()`, which reuses/reinitializes
+  the exact same `struct fd` that fd 1's still-pending dup is chasing
+  via `->dup`. The pending dup then resolves against the *new*
+  (/dev/null) occupant instead of the original fd-2 target.
+
+  **What I tried (session ending 2026-08-17), and why it's reverted:**
+  Tried forcing the real dup2() to happen eagerly, inside
+  `redir_dup()`, right after `fd_dup()` sets up the pending dup, so it
+  captures the source before any later redirection in the same list
+  can reinit its struct. Needed `fdtable_dup(nredir->fd,
+  FDTABLE_FORCE | FDTABLE_CLOSE)` specifically (plain
+  `fdtable_resolve()`/`FDTABLE_MOVE` alone hits a self-referential
+  "redirection cycle detected" error, because the fd being resolved
+  is already linked into its own fdtable slot, and going through
+  `fdtable_wish()`/`fdtable_gap()` has it try to relocate itself out
+  of its own way; `FDTABLE_CLOSE` skips that. `FDTABLE_FORCE` is
+  needed too because fds 0-2 used to never actually get `close()`d
+  when torn down — see the fd≤2-guard writeup below, fixes/186 — so
+  fdtable's bookkeeping believed slot 1 was free while the kernel fd
+  was still open, and only `FDTABLE_FORCE`'s dup2()-retry path could
+  actually reclaim it).
